.\" Manual page for Terminaal. Installed by install.sh to
.\" $XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man1/terminaal.1; render it while editing with
.\" `man ./assets/terminaal.1`.
.TH TERMINAAL 1 "2026-09-24" "Terminaal" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
terminaal \- terminal emulator with a built-in SSH manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B terminaal
.RB [ \-\-connect
.RI [ USER @] HOST ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR SHELL ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR LINE ]
.RB [ \-\-hold ]
.br
.B terminaal \-\-quake
.br
.B terminaal
.RB { \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Terminaal
is a terminal emulator with tabs, split panes, a sidebar for shells, SSH
hosts and keys, an SFTP file tab over the connection of an SSH terminal,
and a settings tab for everything in
.IR config.toml .
.PP
Started without arguments it opens the tabs of the session it saved last
time (see
.B FILES
and
.BR restore_session ),
or one tab with the default shell.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-connect " " [ USER @] HOST
Open the first tab as an SSH connection instead of a local shell.
.I HOST
is the name of a host saved in
.I hosts.toml
or of a
.B Host
block in
.IR ~/.ssh/config .
With
.IR USER @,
that host's login for that user, or else its default login under that
user name. Connecting, host-key questions and password or passphrase
prompts all play out inside the tab.
.TP
.BI \-s " " SHELL
Start the first tab with this shell instead of the configured default.
.I SHELL
is a name as listed in
.I /etc/shells
.RB ( fish )
or a path
.RB ( /usr/bin/fish );
a path is run as given even when it isn't listed.
.TP
.B \-s
Without a shell name: write the installed shells
.RI ( /etc/shells ,
the same list the sidebar shows) to standard output and exit.
.TP
.BI \-c " " LINE
Have the shell run
.I LINE
instead of starting interactively, like
.BR "sh -c" :
the whole line is one argument and the shell evaluates it, so pipes,
redirections and several commands separated by
.B ;
work. The tab closes when the line ends, unless
.B \-\-hold
is given. Together with
.BR \-\-connect ,
the line runs on the server (ssh_config's
.BR RemoteCommand ).
The line is evaluated by a non-interactive shell, which for bash and zsh
means no startup file and none of the aliases Terminaal manages.
.TP
.B \-\-hold
Keep the tab when what it runs has ended, with a last line naming the
exit status \(en over SSH the one the server reports for its command \(en
instead of closing with it. Goes with
.BR \-\-connect ", " \-s " or " \-c :
on its own there is nothing to keep open, since a plain start restores
the saved session.
.TP
.B \-\-quake
Show or hide the drop-down window: the first
.B terminaal \-\-quake
becomes that window, every later one toggles it over a socket in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/terminaal .
It keeps tabs and a session of its own and goes with no other argument.
There are no global hotkeys under Wayland, so bind this command as a
shortcut in the desktop's own settings; Settings \(-> General shows the
command with its full path.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-connect ,
.B \-s
or
.BR \-c ,
the saved session is neither restored nor written: the tab is what the
command line asked for, and what was open last time stays on disk.
.SH KEYS
All of these are configurable under Settings \(-> Shortcuts or in
.B [shortcuts]
of
.IR config.toml .
The defaults:
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
Ctrl+Shift+T@New tab
Ctrl+Shift+W@Close pane (the tab with its last one)
Ctrl+Shift+Alt+W@Close the tab with all its panes
Ctrl+Shift+D@Split right
Ctrl+Shift+Alt+D@Split down
Ctrl+Alt+arrow@Focus the pane in that direction
Ctrl+Shift+Enter@Show the focused pane on its own
Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab@Next, previous tab
Alt+1 ... Alt+9@Tab N
Ctrl+Shift+PageUp/PageDown@Move the tab left/right
Ctrl+Shift+I@Broadcast input to this terminal
Ctrl+Shift+O@Files (SFTP) of this SSH terminal
Ctrl+Shift+B@Sidebar
Ctrl+,@Settings
Ctrl+Shift+P@Command palette
Ctrl+Shift+C, Ctrl+Shift+V@Copy, paste
Ctrl+Shift+Alt+C@Copy the last command's output
Ctrl+Shift+F@Search the scrollback
Ctrl+Shift+Up/Down@Previous, next prompt
Shift+PageUp/PageDown/Home/End@Scroll the scrollback
Ctrl++, Ctrl+-, Ctrl+0@Bigger, smaller, default font size
.TE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/terminaal/config.toml
All settings, written key by key so comments and formatting survive.
.TP
.I ~/.config/terminaal/hosts.toml
Saved SSH hosts with their logins and options. Hosts from
.I ~/.ssh/config
are read as well, and never written to.
.TP
.I ~/.config/terminaal/keys.toml
The key store: paths of key files, or the public key of an agent key.
Nothing secret is ever saved \(en passwords and passphrases are asked
for in the tab.
.TP
.I ~/.config/terminaal/snippets.toml
Your own commands, as buttons in the sidebar and entries in the command
palette, optionally bound to a system or a host and run at startup.
.TP
.I ~/.config/terminaal/themes/
Your own themes, in Alacritty's color format plus an optional
.B [ui]
table. The built-in ones need no file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/terminaal/shell-integration/
Generated startup files that load Terminaal's managed aliases and the
shell integration (OSC 7 and OSC 133) \(en for Terminaal only. Your own
.IR config.fish ", " .bashrc " and " .zshrc
are never touched.
.TP
.I ~/.config/fish/terminaal.fish, ~/.bash_terminaal, $ZDOTDIR/.zsh_terminaal
The aliases, functions and lines the sidebar manages, per shell.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/terminaal/session.toml
The tabs, split layout, shells with their directories and SSH hosts of
the last session;
.I quake\-session.toml
for the drop-down window. Only the first running instance owns it.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/known_hosts
Host keys, as OpenSSH writes them. New entries are appended; the sidebar
can remove the entries of one host, leaving the rest of the file
byte for byte as it was.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log level, e.g.
.BR terminaal=debug " (frame timings) or " terminaal=trace
(the SSH worker's timeline). Logs are English and go to standard error.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell of new tabs unless one is configured or picked with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BR LANGUAGE ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
Decide the interface language (German or English) unless
.B language
is set in
.IR config.toml .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The window was closed, or
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version ", " \-s " without a name or " \-\-quake
did their job.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong, or the host or shell it named wasn't found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR sftp (1)
.PP
The documentation in the source tree's
.I docs/
directory covers all of it in more detail.
